// Reference builds, one per activity per tier, and nothing a player ever reaches.
//
// A tier is a level and an activity: what somebody who has climbed that activity to that level
// has earned, wearing what that level lets them wear. It is what `npm run simulate-activity`
// is pointed at when the question is what the world pays at level ten rather than at level one,
// because a rate is only ever a rate for somebody, and the somebody has to be written down.
//
// Nothing here was reckoned. Each was printed by `npm run tier-build`, which spends the tier's
// experience pool -- one skill climb for each skill the activity uses -- and then hands the whole
// of the activity's kit over and puts on as much of it as the engine allows. So the difference
// between the tiers below is not a decision anybody made about what a tier may wear: it is the
// twelve `requires:` lines in the world, answered. The iron set arrives at ten and the Knight's
// Sword at twenty because that is what they ask for.
//
// A piece that drops with an item level drops with points, and a build that never spends them is a
// character at a fraction of what its own gear allows -- half the attack, at twenty. So every kit
// below is handed the jewel catalogue too, and `--grow` names the stats each activity is climbing
// for. Which jewel goes where is measured rather than chosen: every node and every jewel is applied
// through the growth screen's own doors and the stat is read back, so a jewel written next month is
// tried here by being handed over and nothing in the tool has to learn what it does.
//
// Both spends are floors and not answers. The pool goes evenly across the activity's skills, and
// the points go greedily one piece at a time in the order they went on -- so the first piece on
// takes the jewels it likes, and a corridor that would have paid three nodes later is never
// crossed. Whether the best combat build at a tier pours everything into attack is exactly the
// question a search over these is for. Regenerate any of them with the command above and paste the
// body back; `npm run tier-build -- --help` prints the whole of it.

# info tiers
version: 1.0.0
pack: balance
dependencies:
  core
  combat
  cooking
  crafting
  fishing
  thieving


// --- combat ---

// Leather under bronze under iron, a shield, and the best blade the level has earned. Every
// piece is handed over at every tier and the engine keeps what it will. Grown toward attack first
// and health behind it, which is what a build that means to fight is for.

# save combat-tier-1
